  "findings": "Slow Application and Offer objects have much longer flow times than median objects, but they do not appear to have more events overall. Slow Applications have a median flow time of 3656215.0s versus 1645576.0s for median Applications, a 2.38x ratio, while their mean events per object are slightly lower, 8.31 versus 8.62. Slow Offers have a median flow time of 3283889.0s versus 1372916.0s for median Offers, a 2.58x ratio, with fewer mean events per object, 4.42 versus 4.78. Structurally, slow Applications are disproportionately associated with A_Cancelled activity, with ratio 8.31, and slow Offers are disproportionately associated with O_Cancelled, ratio 2.65, and O_Sent (online only), ratio 1.42. The Application-to-Offer relationship shows a positive correlation between number of linked Offers and Application flow time: Pearson r=0.2202 and Spearman rho=0.1782, both p=0.0. Thus, having more Offers per Application may contribute to longer Application flow times. For Application-to-Workflow links, correlation could not be computed because every Application has exactly one linked Workflow, so Workflow count does not explain variation in slow flow time. The tool output does not isolate repeated participation specifically at the bottleneck activity because the bottleneck activity name was not provided in this subtask; however, the slow-vs-median comparison shows that slow objects do not generally have more total events.",
